+## 2. flags.go
+
+```go
+const flagName = "attach"
+
+// Flag is the repeatable --attach flag.
+type Flag struct {
+    flag   *pflag.Flag
+    values *[]string
+}
+
+// AddFlag registers --attach on cmd and returns a handle to it.
+func AddFlag(cmd *cobra.Command) *Flag
+
+// Changed reports whether --attach was passed.
+func (f *Flag) Changed() bool
+
+// UserAssets validates every value the flag collected.
+func (f *Flag) UserAssets() ([]UserAsset, error)
+```
+
+Register with `cmd.Flags().StringArray` (a string ARRAY, not a string slice, so a comma stays part of a file name), no shorthand. The usage string must be copied verbatim from the assertion in `TestAddFlag` in internal/attachments/flags_test.go. The field named `flag` must hold the `*pflag.Flag` returned by `cmd.Flags().Lookup(flagName)`, since the test reads `attachFlag.flag.Value`, `attachFlag.flag.Shorthand` and `attachFlag.flag.Usage`.
+
+`UserAssets`: return `(nil, nil)` when the flag was not passed. Otherwise, for each value in order:
+
+- `value == ""` -> `errors.New("cannot attach an empty path; --attach needs a file path")`
+- `value == "-"` -> `errors.New("cannot attach standard input; --attach needs a file path")`
+- split it into a path and an alt text at a `#`. A file name may itself contain `#`, so choose the LONGEST prefix of the value that ends at a `#` boundary (or the whole value) and names something `os.Stat` can find; the alt text is whatever follows that `#`. Try the whole value first, then progressively shorter prefixes. If no candidate exists on disk, fall back to splitting at the LAST `#` (and when there is no `#`, the whole value is the path and the alt text is empty), so the resulting `newAsset` call reports a missing-file error naming the path the user most likely meant.
+- call `newAsset(path, alt)` and return `(nil, err)` on the first failure.
+
+Then reject duplicates: if two resolved assets are the same file according to `os.SameFile` on their stat info (this catches a symlink, a hard link and two spellings of one path), return `fmt.Errorf("%s and %s are the same file; attached files must be unique", earlierPath, laterPath)` using the paths exactly as the user typed them, earlier one first. Any error returns a nil slice.
