+
+Every row of those tables must pass. The row comments explain WHY each case is what it is; read them, they will save you from guessing.
+
+## Behaviour summary (the tests are authoritative if this disagrees)
+
+### newAttachableMarkdown
+Validation only, before anything uploads. It parses the markdown and refuses exactly one thing: an attachment whose `RendersAsPlayer` is true that is embedded through a **reference-style image** (`![label][ref]`, `![label][]`, `![label]` resolved by a `[ref]: ./repro.mp4` definition). A video has no inline destination to turn into a bare URL there, so it cannot be embedded that way.
+
+Error message: `cannot embed a video as a reference-style image: ` followed by the offending paths joined with `, ` — each offending argument named once, in the order the arguments were passed. On error it must return the ZERO `attachableMarkdown` value (a test asserts `require.Zero(t, v)`), so refused markdown cannot be used.
+
+A video used as a reference-style **link** (`[label][ref]`) is fine. An inline video embed (`![x](./repro.mp4)`) is fine — the degrade rule below handles it. A reference-style video image inside a code fence is fine. An unused definition for a video is fine.
+
+On success it returns `attachableMarkdown{markdown: markdown, attachmentArgs: attachmentArgs}`. The caller fills in the `URL` of the elements of that slice afterwards and then calls `attachAssetsToMarkdown`, so keep the slice by reference; do not copy the elements.
+
+### attachAssetsToMarkdown
+Returns the rewritten markdown and the arguments that nothing in the markdown referenced (`ToAppend`), so the caller can append them. `ToAppend` keeps the order the arguments were passed. An argument whose `URL` is empty is never rewritten and is never appended (nothing uploaded for it).
+
+#### Matching a destination against an attached file
+A destination is a candidate local path unless it is:
+- something starting with `#` (a fragment/anchor),
+- something starting with `//` (a protocol-relative URL),
+- something with a URL scheme, meaning TWO OR MORE ASCII letters followed by `:` (`mailto:`, `https:`). A ONE-letter scheme like `C:/Users/me/login.png` IS a path, because that is how Windows names a volume.
+
+Candidates match by absolute, cleaned path: resolve both the destination and `arg.Path` against the process working directory and compare. `./login.png` in the markdown therefore matches an argument given as an absolute path, and the other way round.
+
+Before comparing, the destination must be decoded the way markdown spells a path: backslash-escaped punctuation collapses (`./login\(1\).png` is `./login(1).png`, `./a\\b.png` is `./a\b.png`, `<./a\>b.png>` is `./a>b.png`) and percent-encoding decodes (`./Screenshot%202026-08-10%20at%205.38.10%20PM.png` is `./Screenshot 2026-08-10 at 5.38.10 PM.png`). Check empirically what goldmark already did for you before decoding twice. Whitespace inside angle brackets is significant and must be kept: `<` space `login.png` space `>` names a file whose name has those spaces, and is a DIFFERENT file from `login.png`.
+
+#### What gets rewritten
+The rewrite must be a byte-level edit of the original source: everything except the destination (or, for a degraded/promoted video, the node) stays exactly as the author wrote it. Titles survive in every spelling (`"t"`, `'t'`, `(t)`, and a title containing escaped quotes). Formatting inside alt text survives. CRLF line endings survive.
+
+- **Image node whose argument is an image**: only the destination changes. `![alt](./login.png)` becomes `![alt](URL)`.
+- **Link node (any argument)**: stays a link, only the destination changes.
+- **Image node whose argument is a video**:
+  - If the image is the ONLY content of a `Paragraph` (leading/trailing whitespace in that paragraph aside), replace the whole image with the bare URL, keeping that surrounding whitespace: `  ![x](./repro.mp4)  ` becomes `  URL  `. A bare URL on its own is the only form GitHub renders as a player. This applies inside a blockquote, a nested blockquote, a blockquote inside a list item, and a LOOSE list item — all of which contain a real Paragraph.
+  - Otherwise it degrades to a link: `![label](./repro.mp4)` becomes `[label](URL)`. This covers an image sharing a paragraph with other text (including across a soft line break), a heading, and a TIGHT list item (which holds a `TextBlock`, not a `Paragraph`). When the label is empty, use `arg.Alt` as the link text with `]` escaped as `\]`. References nested inside the label must still be rewritten in place — do not rebuild the node from stale source bytes.
+  - When dropping the leading `!` of a degrade, look at the character before it. If it is a `!` that is not itself already escaped, escape that one (`Watch this!![demo](...)` becomes `Watch this\![demo](URL)`), otherwise a new image embed re-forms. A `!` that is already escaped (`\!`) stays exactly as it is. A `]` before the degrade needs no escaping. A degrade at the very start of the document needs no escaping.
+
+#### Reference-style definitions
+A usage written as `![x][ref]`, `[x][ref]`, `![ref][]` or `![ref]` takes its destination from a `[ref]: ./login.png "Title"` definition line. Rewrite the DEFINITION once; every usage of that label follows.
+
+Rule for which definitions to rewrite: rewrite a definition whose destination matches an argument that at least one image or link node in the document actually resolved to. Consequences the tests check:
+- A definition nothing uses is left alone and its file is appended instead (editing it would leave the file invisible).
+- A SECOND definition carrying the same destination as a used one IS rewritten too, even though nothing uses that second label.
+- A definition for a file nobody attached is left alone.
+- A definition inside a code fence, or inside an inline code span, is not a definition.
+- One definition used by both a link and an image is rewritten once.
+- A definition keeps its title, including a title on the following line.
+- An angle-bracketed definition destination is replaced whole; an escaped `>` inside belongs to the name; a `<` that is never closed leaves the definition alone (and the file is appended, because goldmark then sees no definition at all and nothing resolves).
+- A tab between the destination and the title ends the destination.
+- A definition label containing an escaped `]` (for example `[a\]: b]: ./login.png`) must be found: skip over `\]` when looking for the `]:` that ends the label.
+- A definition on one line inside a blockquote is rewritten; a definition whose destination continued onto the NEXT line of a blockquote is left alone and the file appended, because the bytes that follow `[shot]:` on its own line are not the path.
+- CRLF definitions are rewritten.
+
+Note that goldmark consumes link reference definitions during block parsing and does not put them in the AST, so you will have to locate them in the source yourself (scan for a definition line outside any code block or code span, honouring up to three leading spaces and any blockquote or list markers).
+
+#### Things that must be left exactly as written
+- Anything inside a fenced code block, an indented code block or an inline code span. Note a code span can contain a `](path)` that an earlier `[` outside it could otherwise pair with, so a byte scan must skip a code span's bytes.
+- A remote URL, an anchor, a `mailto:`, a protocol-relative URL.
+- A local path nobody attached.
+- A destination that markdown does not actually parse as a destination: an unbalanced opening `(` inside the path, a bare space in an unbracketed path, an unclosed `<`. The file is appended in those cases.
+- A `](` earlier in the block that is NOT a valid link tail (an unterminated title, a tail with no closing parenthesis, a destination followed by a word and then `)`, a destination containing a line break) must be skipped rather than swallowing the real reference that follows.
+
+Balanced parentheses inside an unbracketed destination belong to the path (`./f(1).png`, `./f((1)(2)).png`); an escaped `)` belongs to the path; an unbalanced `)` ends the destination (`![a](./f).png)` has destination `./f` and the trailing `.png)` falls out as literal text — GitHub renders it the same way).
+
+#### Structures that must be told apart
+- An image nested inside a link: `[![badge](./login.png)](./repro.mp4)` — both halves rewritten, each by its own rule.
+- A thumbnail linking to its own file: both halves rewritten.
+- A video embed whose alt text contains a link to an image, alone in a paragraph: the whole embed becomes the bare URL, and the swallowed image argument is reported in `ToAppend` rather than silently dropped.
+- The same file referenced twice, in one paragraph or in two, is rewritten in both places and uploaded once.
+
+### isSingleImage(markdown, url) bool
+True only when the markdown is exactly one paragraph holding exactly one image node, that image points at `url`, and nothing else is in the document — no trailing prose, no second image, no second paragraph, and no alt text that closed the image early and stole the destination. Escaped brackets inside the alt text are fine. Empty markdown is false.
+
+## How to work
+
+Write the implementation, then iterate against the tests until everything is green:
+
+```
+go test ./internal/attachments/ -run 'TestNewAttachableMarkdown' 
+go test ./internal/attachments/ -run 'TestIsSingleImage'
+go test ./internal/attachments/ -run 'TestAttachAssetsToMarkdown'
+go test ./internal/attachments/ -run 'TestAttachAssetsToMarkdownFixture'
+go test ./internal/attachments/
+```
+
+A suggested shape, which you are free to depart from if you find something better:
+1. Parse the source once with goldmark into an AST over the source bytes.
+2. Walk the AST collecting every `*ast.Image` and `*ast.Link` whose destination resolves to an attached argument that has a URL, recording for each: the argument, whether it is an image or a link, whether it is reference-style, and for an image-of-a-video whether its parent is a `Paragraph` it is alone in.
+   To tell a reference-style node from an inline one, find the node's label end in the source (walk to its last descendant that carries a `text.Segment`, then scan forward at bracket depth zero to the `]` that closes the label) and look at the byte after it: `(` means inline, anything else means reference-style. An image with no children at all can be treated as inline; no test writes `![][ref]`.
+3. Collect edits as (byte range, replacement) pairs, then apply them to the source from the last offset to the first so earlier offsets stay valid. For an edit inside a region that another edit replaces (a reference inside a degraded video label), apply the inner edits first and build the outer replacement from the already-edited bytes.
+4. Locate definitions with a separate source scan that skips code blocks and code spans.
+5. Report any argument with a URL that no edit consumed, in argument order.
+
+Keep the code readable: this lives in the gh CLI and will be reviewed. Doc comments on the package-level identifiers, short helpers with explanatory names, comments where a rule is surprising.
+
